# Reversing a Singly Linked List in C

Here's a complete implementation that reverses a singly linked list in place using an iterative approach.

## Struct Definition

```c
typedef struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;
```

## Reverse Function

```c
Node *reverse_list(Node *head)
{
    Node *prev = NULL;
    Node *curr = head;
    Node *next = NULL;

    while (curr != NULL) {
        next = curr->next;  /* Save the next node */
        curr->next = prev;  /* Reverse the link */
        prev = curr;        /* Advance prev */
        curr = next;        /* Advance curr */
    }

    return prev;  /* prev is now the new head */
}
```

## How It Works

The algorithm uses three pointers that walk through the list:

1. **`prev`** — the node that will become the new `next` for the current node (starts as `NULL` because the original head becomes the new tail).
2. **`curr`** — the node currently being processed.
